# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TaxElixir — environment
#
# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored.
# Only the variables below are read by the app; there is nothing else to set.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Supabase — Dashboard > Project Settings > API
# Required. Without these the site still builds and browses, but the blog and
# resources render empty and /upload and /admin show a "not configured" notice.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Canonical origin for <link rel="canonical">, sitemap.xml, robots.txt and
# JSON-LD. Optional — defaults to https://www.taxelixir.com.
#
# Set it on preview/staging deploys, otherwise those environments emit canonical
# tags pointing at production and can get themselves de-indexed in favour of it.
# No trailing slash.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://www.taxelixir.com


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT set here — email notifications
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Enquiries, uploads, guide downloads and newsletter signups are emailed by the
# `lead-notification` Supabase Edge Function, which is triggered by a database
# webhook — not by Next.js. Its secrets live in Supabase, not in .env.local:
#
#   supabase functions deploy lead-notification
#   supabase secrets set \
#     SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com \
#     SMTP_PORT=465 \
#     SMTP_USER=leads@taxelixir.com \
#     SMTP_PASS=<app password> \
#     NOTIFICATION_EMAIL=info@taxelixir.com
#
# Putting SMTP credentials in .env.local will do nothing — the function cannot
# read them. See docs/DATABASE.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT needed — service-role key
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is deliberately no SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY here. Admin file downloads
# are signed using the logged-in admin's own session against a storage RLS
# policy, so the service-role key is never required by the app. Keeping a key
# that bypasses all RLS out of the deployment is worth more than the
# convenience of having it available.
